---

# 5. `docs/agents/README.md`

This file is the entry point for the AGNES Agent system.

Document:

## Architecture

```text
Producer Supervisor
        │
        ├── Script Writer
        ├── Character Designer
        ├── Director
        ├── Production Planner
        ├── Shot Producer
        ├── Continuity Reviewer
        └── Editor
```

Explain that these are exactly eight fixed role types.

Do not add more roles in this phase.

## Core operating model

Explain clearly:

```text
Agent decides.
Capability authorizes.
Service / Workflow executes.
Artifact carries durable results.
```

## Conversation vs task

Distinguish:

```text
Direct Conversation
```

from:

```text
Delegated AgentTask
```

A direct conversation is user ↔ specialist interaction.

A delegated task is Producer → Specialist structured work.

They may use the same role and domain knowledge, but delegation must remain traceable through AgentTask.

## Runtime truth vs role documentation

Be explicit:

* `AgentRegistry` / `CapabilityRegistry` are the executable authorization truth.
* Role documents describe the intended operating contract.
* Documentation must never claim permissions that the runtime does not grant.
* If documentation and executable permissions diverge, report and fix the mismatch deliberately rather than silently inventing behavior.

---

# 6. Use one exact Role Contract template for all eight Agents

Every role file must use the same headings.

Use this structure:

```text
# <Agent Name>

## 1. Stable identity
## 2. Mission
## 3. Owns
## 4. Explicitly does NOT own
## 5. Accepted task types
## 6. Tasks it must reject or redirect
## 7. Scope
## 8. Required context
## 9. Read permissions
## 10. Write / execution permissions
## 11. Current implemented capabilities
## 12. Reserved future capabilities
## 13. Inputs
## 14. Structured outputs / artifacts
## 15. Definition of done
## 16. Failure / blocker behavior
## 17. Direct Conversation mode
## 18. Delegated AgentTask mode
## 19. Upstream dependencies
## 20. Downstream consumers
## 21. Delegation / escalation rules
## 22. Human-control boundary
## 23. Canonical / Workspace boundary
## 24. Model / tool policy
## 25. UI context
## 26. Examples
## 27. Anti-examples
## 28. Current implementation status
```

Do not turn these into personality biographies.

These are operating contracts.

---

# 7. Producer Supervisor contract

The Producer is the global coordinator.

Mission:

> Understand project-level user intent, inspect overall project/workflow state, decompose work, choose the correct Specialist, create traceable AgentTasks, and synthesize results.

Owns:

```text
coordination
task decomposition
specialist selection
AgentTask creation
project-level progress understanding
result synthesis
```

Does NOT own:

```text
screenplay writing
character design
storyboard editing
production-plan authoring
shot generation
quality review
composition details
```

The Producer must not become a universal domain editor.

Direct business mutation should remain extremely limited.

The Producer should normally reach Specialist-domain capabilities through delegation.

Document the current real Producer permissions exactly.

Current delegation remains:

```text
Producer → Specialist
```

Do not document Specialist → Specialist as currently available.

---

# 8. Script Writer contract

Mission:

> Own narrative/story structure and screenplay-level creative intent.

Owns conceptually:

```text
story
scene narrative
story structure
dialogue / narrative intent where supported
character participation relationships
```

Does NOT own:

```text
shot composition
camera strategy
production planning
image/video generation
quality review
final composition
```

Very important:

Do not describe screenplay editing capabilities as implemented unless they actually are.

If current capability is inspect-only, say so explicitly.

Future screenplay structural writes must still use a controlled business/revision path rather than raw project.json writes.

---

# 9. Character Designer contract

Mission:

> Own character identity, visual definition, references, and character-specific consistency decisions.

Owns conceptually:

```text
character description
appearance intent
character reference assets
character visual consistency
```

Does NOT own:

```text
story structure
shot structure
production-plan decisions
video production
composition
```

Clearly distinguish:

```text
Character Designer decides/designs the character
```

from:

```text
Shot Producer uses character references while producing a shot
```

Do not allow both roles to independently redefine character identity.

---

# 10. Director contract

The Director is now the first fully proven Specialist.

Use the real AI-6D behavior as the reference implementation for what a strong role contract looks like.

Mission:

> Translate scene/shot creative intent into reviewable Storyboard decisions.

Owns:

```text
shot structure
shot description
storyboard pacing
insert / update / delete / reorder intent
```

The Director does NOT own:

```text
production strategy
asset generation
final quality acceptance
final composition
```

The canonical protection rule must be explicit:

```text
Director
→ semantic proposal
→ existing ChangeOperation / ChangeSet
→ Preview / Impact
→ Creative Workspace
```

Never:

```text
Director
→ directly overwrite canonical shots
```

Document the currently implemented `storyboard.propose_change` capability accurately.

Use Director as the gold-standard example for future Specialist integration.

---

# 11. Production Planner contract

This contract is especially important because AI-6E will implement it next.

Mission:

> Convert an already-decided Shot into a concrete, valid, executable Production Plan.

The Planner answers:

> **How should this Shot be produced?**

The Planner does NOT answer:

> **What should the Shot creatively be?**

Owns:

```text
production strategy
continuity strategy
opening-source strategy
ending strategy
reference-source decisions
prompt / generation strategy
production-plan fields
```

Does NOT own:

```text
Storyboard creative redesign
Character identity
actual asset generation
video acceptance/rejection
final composition
```

This boundary must be extremely explicit:

```text
Director decides the shot.
Planner decides how to produce that shot.
Shot Producer executes the plan.
```

If the Planner discovers that the Shot itself is creatively invalid or underspecified, it must return a structured blocker/escalation rather than silently redesigning the Shot.

The appropriate conceptual upstream owner is Director.

However:

**Specialist → Specialist delegation is still not implemented.**

Therefore document this as an escalation relationship, not as an executable delegation chain.

For current AI-6E.0, do NOT implement `production_plan.create`.

Document it for the next phase.

---

# 12. Shot Producer contract

Mission:

> Execute an approved Production Plan using the existing deterministic generation pipeline.

Owns execution of production capabilities such as, when actually implemented:

```text
first frame
last frame
image generation
video generation
candidate generation
retry execution
production jobs
```

Does NOT own:

```text
rewriting Storyboard intent
inventing a different Production Plan
changing Character identity
deciding final quality acceptance
```

The key boundary is:

```text
Planner = decides how
Shot Producer = executes how
```

If a Production Plan is invalid/stale/missing, Shot Producer must not improvise a new creative strategy.

It should return a structured blocker.

Do not implement these execution capabilities during AI-6E.0.

---

# 13. Continuity Reviewer contract

Mission:

> Evaluate generated results against creative intent, production plan, and continuity expectations.

Owns:

```text
quality findings
continuity findings
accept/reject recommendation
retry recommendation
structured review
```

Does NOT own:

```text
silent Storyboard rewrites
silent Production Plan rewrites
silent asset regeneration
final project mutation
```

The Reviewer should produce structured findings rather than secretly fixing everything itself.

Clearly distinguish future concepts:

```text
Review finding
Retry proposal
```

from:

```text
actual retry execution
```

Actual retry execution belongs to Shot Producer unless a later explicit capability contract changes this.

---

# 14. Editor contract

Mission:

> Assemble already-produced creative material into the final deliverable.

Owns:

```text
composition
selected versions
audio/subtitle/composition state
export
```

Does NOT own:

```text
story rewriting
character redesign
storyboard creative redesign
production-plan authoring
asset regeneration
```

If the Editor discovers an upstream creative problem, it should report a blocker rather than silently acting as Director.

Document existing composition/export capabilities accurately.

---

# 15. Collaboration protocol

Create `COLLABORATION_PROTOCOL.md`.

This is not a generic multi-agent essay.

It must describe AGNES specifically.

Define the canonical conceptual chain:

```text
User / Producer
        ↓
Script Writer / Character Designer
        ↓ structured project state

Director
        ↓
Storyboard / Director Proposal

Production Planner
        ↓
Production Plan

Shot Producer
        ↓
Assets / Production Run

Continuity Reviewer
        ↓
Review Report / Retry Proposal

Editor
        ↓
Composition / Final Export
```

Not every project must invoke every role.

Do not imply a rigid mandatory waterfall.

---

# 16. Artifact-first handoff

Make this a core rule.

Agents should share durable structured state such as:

```text
Project
Workspace
ChangeSet
AgentTask
Storyboard
ProductionPlan
ProductionRun
Asset
ReviewResult
Composition state
```

They should NOT primarily hand work to each other by copying long chat transcripts.

A Specialist should return:

```text
summary
structured_result
artifact_refs
warnings
suggested_next_actions
```

where appropriate.

Do not invent new persistence formats merely for this documentation phase.

Use existing structures where they already exist.

---

# 17. Task acceptance contract

Every role must define what happens when it receives a task outside its domain.

The Agent must not opportunistically perform another Specialist's job.

Use one of:

```text
complete
blocked
needs_upstream_change
needs_specialist
not_supported
```

conceptually.

Do NOT necessarily add these as new runtime enums in AI-6E.0.

Document the semantics first.

If the current runtime already has appropriate structured status values, reuse their terminology.

Do not expand runtime models merely to make the documentation prettier.

---

# 18. Delegation boundary

Current executable rule remains:

```text
Producer → Specialist
```

Current executable rule remains:

```text
Specialist → Specialist = forbidden
```

Document future collaboration such as:

```text
Director → Planner
Planner → Shot Producer
Reviewer → Shot Producer
```

only as conceptual upstream/downstream relationships.

Do not implement recursive delegation.

Do not implement automatic task chaining.

That belongs to a later explicit phase.

---

# 19. Direct conversation mode

Every Specialist can be accessed directly by the user.

Document that direct conversation does not magically expand its permissions.

For example:

If the user directly tells Production Planner:

> "Delete Shot 04."

Planner should explain that Storyboard structure belongs to Director rather than pretending to own the operation.

Likewise:

If the user tells Shot Producer:

> "Make this scene slower and add a close-up."

Shot Producer should not silently redesign the Storyboard.

Role boundaries apply equally in:

```text
direct user conversation
```

and:

```text
Producer-delegated task
```

---

# 20. Canonical / Creative Workspace rules

Every role document must explicitly state its mutation boundary.

At minimum distinguish:

```text
read-only inspection

domain business state

Creative Workspace revision

canonical project
```

No Agent gains raw canonical write authority from documentation.

Director's existing Workspace / ChangeSet path remains the reference pattern.

Do not implement canonical merge.

Do not implement "make official."

Do not invent a new revision model.

---

# 21. Current vs future capability status

Every role document must include a table such as:

```text
Capability | Role relationship | Current status | Notes
```

Use only real Capability IDs from the current registry.

Clearly mark:

```text
implemented
reserved
not_implemented
```

Do not invent capability IDs without strong reason.

If a capability is conceptually desirable but does not exist, place it under a separate "future consideration" section rather than pretending it is already registered.

---

# 22. Model policy is secondary to role policy

Document that ModelProfile does not define role authority.

Changing a reasoning model must not change:

```text
Agent permissions
domain ownership
write boundaries
delegation permissions
completion contract
```

A stronger model is still the same role.

Image/video models are tools, not Agents.

Account selection is infrastructure policy, not role identity.

Do not redesign ModelProfile in this phase.

---

# 23. Keep runtime prompts concise

Inspect the existing short Agent system prompts.

Do NOT paste the entire role documents into runtime system prompts.

The runtime prompt should remain concise and high-signal.

Only correct an existing system prompt if it materially contradicts the new documented role contract.

If changes are necessary, keep them minimal:

```text
identity
mission
owns
does-not-own
scope
tool/write boundary
output expectation
```

Do not convert the runtime prompts into multi-thousand-token manuals.

If no runtime prompt change is necessary, leave the code unchanged.

